# Photos -- what the viewer may NAME. The other half is photos.caps.
#
# READ-ONLY everywhere, deliberately. A viewer has no reason to write: it does
# not edit, it does not cache, it keeps no state worth persisting. Declaring
# that here means a bug in it cannot damage a picture even in principle, which
# is a stronger statement than "the code has no write calls in it" -- that is a
# property of today's code, and this is a property of the process.
#
# EVERY PREFIX HERE MUST BE ONE THE SESSION CAN ALREADY NAME. The kernel
# resolves each one in the PARENT's namespace at spawn time, so a prefix the
# desktop does not hold is not a narrower grant -- it is a spawn that fails
# with -2 before the program is ever loaded. The session holds
#   ro /system, ro /data/apps, rw /home/<user>, rw /run
# and this asked for `ro /data/pictures`, which is outside it: the launcher
# reported "spawn FAILED: -2" and nothing opened. settings.ns carries a comment
# about making exactly this mistake with `rw /data`. Hence $HOME, which is
# where the pictures live now anyway.

ro /system                  # its font and chrome resources
ro $HOME                    # the user's pictures, including $HOME/Pictures
